\begin_layout Standard
The search space of the trees is limited by the tree depth and the number
 of nodes.
 This avoids overvitting, the evaluation of the candidates and the evolution
 process is faster and the number of nodes can be additionally weighted.
 Instead of storing only the best candidate, a Pareto Front is created,
 on which the fittest candidates for the respective complexity are located.
 This allows the user to select the desired ratio between fitness and complexity.
 For this purpose, the best candidates are put on a Pareto front with the
 fittest candidate depending on their complexity.
 As complexity results from the number of nodes of a tree.
 Random trees are created by choosing a random depth, up to which the trees
 are built up with random operators.
 A branch can be build in the full and grow mode.
 With the full method, open nodes are always filled up with operators, with
 the grow method terminal nodes are inserted by chance (50%).
 Trees, which were built with the full-method, not only have more nodes
 than trees of the grow-method, but the branching factor is also much bigger
 which leads to an early combination of leaf nodes to complex expressions.
 In grow trees, partial expressions can be considered in isolation for a
 longer time, which can lead to an easier understanding.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:grow-vs-full-method"

\end_inset

 Both approaches can generate important components, so they were used 50%
 each (half ramped mode).
 The nodes are randomly selected from different distributions.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
To obtain the most useful set of constants possible, these are chosen at
 random from 100 variables of the data set, Gaussian-distributed integers
 and Gaussian-distributed floating point values.
 
\begin_inset Formula $X\sim\mathcal{N}(\mu,\sigma^{2})$
\end_inset

 With the help of the evolutionary mutation of the Gaussian filters, the
 constants of the best trees are additionally modified to fine-tune.
 For the runs of this work, a Gaussian filter with a standard deviation
 of 0.1 was applied.
 (
\begin_inset Formula $X\sim\mathcal{N}(x,0.1)$
\end_inset

xx)
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

