\begin_layout Standard
How explainable a solution is depends on the effort needed for a person
 to gain enough understanding to trust the approach.
 An explanation should thus be provided for the gap between a person who
 has knowledge causally based on paradigms and an unmanageable, unknown
 logic, without relying solely on statistical tests.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
The Trusted Domain Expert
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The pinnacle of explainable or interpretable solutions can therefore be
 regarded as the heuristics created by man himself.
 In many areas, it is therefore up to domain experts to find solution strategies.
 The main reasons to trust an expert over a machine learning approach are:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Large pool of qualified previous knowledge
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

In the case of complex problems, it is possible to build on the knowledge
 of the whole of mankind that has been safely acquired.
 In the case of a RL problem, it is always necessary to start from scratch,
 which is why there are many more potential sources of error.
 Humans do not like to learn and try to use already learned strategies.
 In order to develop a strategy, knowledge that has already been acquired
 is used where possible.
 Here the human being can also fall back on a great wealth of knowledge.
 A well-known example of such a mastered ability is the recognition of shapes
 and faces, which man perfected as a baby to such an extent that interpersonal
 explanations are no longer necessary.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Efficient and secure learning process, Implicit logic 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

Humans have protective mechanisms at their disposal, which have developed
 through the evolutionary process of humans and accompany every learning
 process.
 Among other things, it enables us to recognize and avoid dangers, which
 requires caution and the recognition of causal relationships.
 We can therefore be sure that our entire pattern of action excludes life-threat
ening damage for us.
 Computers, unlike humans, rely on random exploration, which affects both
 startup parameters and exploration.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Explicit logic through the formulation in a language
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

Heuristics and explanations are formulated in already defined languages
 and can be checked for their truthfulness.
 due to the similar thought processes it is possible for humans to put themselve
s in another person's shoes and convey information in an even pedagogically
 valuable way.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Puzzle example
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let's consider, for example, the game Puzzle as an NP-hard problem, where
 a whole picture has to be assembled from a pile of pieces.
 Most people know how to recognize colors, that you first have to turn puzzle
 pieces to the colored side and that you should use the picture on the box
 as a reference point.
 A popular tip for beginners is therefore that you should start with the
 edge of the puzzle.
 This is helpful, because the learning process in humans usually consists
 of the composition of many comprehensibly strung together steps.
\end_layout

