\begin_layout Section
Explainability with Familiarity
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this part we discuss possibilities to make measurable explanations.
 As a result, the Tree Edit Distance (TED) is introduced as a measure of
 familiarity that can be used in GP.
 Furthermore, limitations and possible improvements are discussed.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The explainability distance to the potential candidates are resulted in
 the following considerations for a complexity measurement: 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "xaiMeasurementRequirements"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Prerequisites for a distance measure 
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $d(x,y)\ge0$
\end_inset

 (non-negativity)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $d(x,y)=0\Leftrightarrow x=y$
\end_inset

 (identity of indiscernibles) 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $d(x,y)=d(y,x)$
\end_inset

 (symmetry) 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $d(x,y)\le d(x,z)+d(z,y)$
\end_inset

 (triangle inequality) 
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Measure small differences
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Univariate or low-dimansional multivariate formula which is based on a few
 paradigms
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Not fundamentally limiting the Evolution process
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Tree Edit Distance as Familiarity measure 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The tree edit distance (TED) meets all these requirements and is also a
 well-known problem from theoretical computer science.
 It measures the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

distance from two labeled ordered trees T and T' by the minimum cost sequence
 of edit operations needed to transform T into T'
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "tedOldestPaper"
literal "true"

\end_inset

 Edits are the elemental changes 
\begin_inset Formula $\{\text{delete},\text{insert,\text{modify}\}}$
\end_inset

 that can be applied to a tree.
 Thanks to extensive research in this field, the algorithms for calculating
 the TED have been greatly improved in performance and robustness.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "tedAPTED1,tedAPTED2"
literal "true"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Float figure
wide false
sideways false
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename img/inkscape/TED_operators.pdf
	lyxscale 60

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Caption Standard

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Possible TED changes on a tree: insert (green), delete (red), modify (yellow).
 If the position of a node in a branch changes, only the remaining changes
 are evaluated.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "fig:TED_explain_operators"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Weaknesses and conceivable improvements
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For the experiments the TED was used in its simplest form described above
 due to keeping it simple.
 Nevertheless, it is worth mentioning here a few points which could improve
 the result even further:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Weighted TED
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

The mapping of the necessary edits can be weighted according to preferences.
 This can concern the type of edit (e.g.
 not to evaluate delete operators) or the node types, so that operators
 of the same family (e.g.
 angle operators) or even individually can be weighted.
 The weighting of the nodes is not a trivial task with the TED.
 Therefore the weighted mapping of a tree transformation must be calculated,
 which is not unique and also leaves open questions about how weightings
 should affect the respective changes.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Weighting changes to a more difficult or more simple node? 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Adding a regular complexity measure (possibly as a new dimension of the
 Pareto front)
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Preferred Counting of operators?
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

The structure of a tree is essentially determined by the order of its operators.
 The leaf-node, especially the constants, are in this case with a relative
 high part in the weight of the TED, although the changes are often only
 small adaptations without substantial modification.
 Therefore constants should be much less important.
 Use of incomplete structures as origin (e.g.
 missing terminal nodes).
 The occurrence of observations could be weighted separately with a relatively
 high value to force a reduced use of them.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Use of several known origins in one run, for example the smallest or medium
 TED is used.
 This way, several understandable approaches could be compared directly
 for their extensibility or several partial solutions could be introduced
 separately.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
In its raw form, the TED is a factor that slows down many changes without
 the need for them.
 This is caused, for example, by the random generation of trees that always
 have the same normal complexity, but not necessarily the same editing distance.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Fixed Nodes
\begin_inset CommandInset label
