\begin_layout Standard
The intuition in this allocation is that values greater or smaller than
 the maximum values are to be seen as an extremely strong tendency towards
 a particular decision.
 Results 20 or 1.6 would both be assigned to Action 2, but 20 would have
 the far stronger tendency.
 If a wrong decision is made, for example 0.4 and -20, it will not be scored
 either.
 The aim is to find a mathematical formula that produces moderate results
 and does not produce overfitted extreme values even in special cases.
 If the action area has a minimum and maximum value, the intuition considering
 results outside the action area are interpreted as belonging strongly to
 the limit value.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A disadvantage of the automatic limitation is that extremely high values
 in genotype and phenotype can occur.
 The explicit restriction is nevertheless important, because otherwise a
 central aspect of the GP process is used to implicitly implement this barrier
 in each candidate.
 This is expressed for example in candidates with five-digit exponents,
 whose values are not comprehensible for humans and also stand for classical
 overfitting.
 The risk of making gross mistakes in unknown observations is very high.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
d_{bounded}\begin{cases}
0 & d(\min(\max(x_{ik},a_{\min}),a_{\max}),x_{jk})=0\\
\min(d,\text{penalty}) & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset

Here, a second measure, which aims to produce moderate values, was mixed
 with the main measure, which should indicate the distance to the target
 solution.
 The second measure automatically becomes the KO criterion for candidates,
 single wrong points distort the regression error beyond recognition.
 This slowed down the training process extremely, furthermore, the most
 effective solution was not a formula that only generates moderate values,
 but the use of the min and max function.
\end_layout

