\begin_layout Standard
By generating the data online, an implicit selection of the relevant states
 is automatically carried out.
 An explicit selection or extension of the data by the user is not possible,
 because without an understanding of the problem no evaluation of the data
 is possible without influencing the result significantly.
 Comparatively poor runs can, for example, represent a very serious but
 well solved special case or may have been strongly influenced by chance.
 It is also conceivable to evaluate data for still unknown areas, which
 could include observation combinations that could not occur at all.
 Although the extensions are mainly aimed at the differences to the expert
 programme, no selection should be made here either, since the data already
 correctly made represent an important part of the phenotypic similarity
 to the Origin.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Applicability of the approach
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If the regression error of the expert policy is too large, it may deviate
 too much from the black box for an optimization to succeed.
 This would mean that the two approaches differ fundamentally.
 Improvements that can be found may show differences, but will most likely
 fall within a suboptimal range of the solution space.
 This is supported by a sparse state space.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If the regression error does not become small enough despite a manageable
 initial difference, the target solution might be too complex to be approximated
 with GP.
 This does not mean that a good solution cannot be found with GP, but it
 might require an online training.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
xxx As a black box a convergent approach should be preferred to a volatile
 one (like PSO) if possible, because a solution might be too specific.
\end_layout

