\begin_layout Section
Factors supporting the explainability
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Beim erstellen zufälliger Äste gibt es trotz fehlendem Anhaltspunkt Möglichkeite
n die Änderungen in ihrer Erklärbarkeit zu unterstützen.
 Eine wichtige Eigenschaft für den Menschen ist dabei die Mustererkennung.
 Eine Problemlösung sollte möglichst aus vielen kleinen, austauschbaren
 und als Muster erkennbaren Teilen bestehen (Divide and conquer).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In machine learning there are many clustering methods for subdivision; in
 humans, in contrast to if-distinctions play an important role.
 Although it is the most complex function with 3 input parameters, it creates
 more structure than all other operators because its child trees are not
 combined but can be considered separately.
 Nevertheless, the subtrees must have a relation to each other in order
 to harmonize together.
 This combination creates encapsulated programs in a clear way that humans
 can understand more easily.
 It is always to be welcomed when these are contained in programs, but this
 should not be artificially forced, as they are not useful at every point.
 Therefore, the Eureka weighting (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:EUREKA-GP-complexities"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

) is not suitable for familiarity-based GP.
 The if statement is already penalized by its high branching factor, which
 should not be further supported by a higher weighting.
 Observations have another special role with this, since the only task of
 the GP process is to map their values to an output.
 Observations that are not part of the solution are either irrelevant or
 their information can be sufficiently replaced by other observations, why
 they should be considered in isolation to keep the permutations of their
 influences low.
\end_layout

